 {:db/id "q-87305" :question/id #uuid "1e20e394-569b-5fe6-8d7e-276b93ef613e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87305" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{1}{15}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{5},$ find $P(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-87305/ma-source-field/8dc4e20554173843908a81be2fc3cb0f223f6a66b8a1f787e46ceb490f4d1fa8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
