 {:db/id "q-87893" :question/id #uuid "e5f64ef7-fc02-5b6d-a8c5-440eac71e226" :question/math-academy-id "q-87893" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x}+6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {3}^{1-5x} & >-6 \\\\ {3}^{1-5x} & >-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{3}^{1-5x}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality ${3}^{1-5x}>-10$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-87893/ma-source-field/13901bdb2bfe9037e12ea8fbe7505a2ec9c995040e2979b634212065ac0ca71b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
