 {:db/id "q-87910" :question/id #uuid "7bcc27a5-b987-5302-94f2-988d56293740" :question/math-academy-id "q-87910" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c19425664f79df1f46863f59268ebd27e20defce41b21e877926bbcadc9e5c3.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=7\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $42{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{7\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 7CD & =84 \\\\ CD & =12\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/00/0014faf05eaf15d70f885802e5b820b0c72d42a96f400442f827ed229de8b240.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 12}{2} \\\\  & =30{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-87910/ma-source-field/f3271f7b1ec12adf4ad7a8bd77b9a1e31f28dcb44419290518188cea7ec60862"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
