 {:db/id "q-90725" :question/id #uuid "ec0270d1-70c2-5347-87eb-345d880dbd64" :question/math-academy-id "q-90725" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-90725/ma-source-field/fa9f3a7f66c24c1f95fb491c63c4925089489dd25f82b3f306a959e14665be8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
