 {:db/id "q-91177" :question/id #uuid "fe1d7acc-f95f-567e-b05e-9ad830eda4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-91177" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 9 & -11 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}$\nII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -29 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & -15 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 9 & -11 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-91177/ma-source-field/1e1b60e787d26162f63e5fd5d2d9189e3bd497f6bb928dc307a35bcd93e314bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
