 {:db/id "q-91212" :question/id #uuid "b9ef8afb-c422-57bc-a155-4bf0fc567ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-91212" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & -20 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & -24 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-3{C}_{2}$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{12}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -16 & 8 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 9 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-3{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 8 & -16 & -20 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -17 & -24 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{3}:={C}_{3}-2{C}_{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-91212/ma-source-field/1f9f9cb302f263e8e71916f91b96bfa6fa0e9b12e81018790d4a559cbcdf3cc7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
