 {:db/id "q-93687" :question/id #uuid "9bdcc65d-ace5-50fe-a859-cbc9e6149544" :question/math-academy-id "q-93687" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=4{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x},$ we have that $\\lambda =-5$ is the double root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-5x}.$  In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (25{x}^{2}-20x+2){e}^{-5x}+10(\\alpha (2x-5{x}^{2}){e}^{-5x})+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-20x+2+20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{-5x} & =4{e}^{-5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93687/ma-source-field/03b9f335ae1f52f05f72092f25b8a46e4037bd4434cf2291f03fe8e82bcfef63"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
