 {:db/id "q-93785" :question/id #uuid "311b05cd-8042-5d05-b094-868c3c494826" :question/math-academy-id "q-93785" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha +(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n\\alpha x+(2\\alpha +\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93785/ma-source-field/eee1ef4239be2ba5c80c891098d087dba6c3f7aee90b3f83d0e646e87779fc4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
