 {:db/id "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae8a466-b526-5117-aeba-5c0fc28144b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/29cc5d4b73370438673fc9487fee70cc4ff01bd6c32b22ca58f221f5a3691b94" "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/56f884d814aab61b37ad1c11934f96d8d2f7a8aa3ed42a73c9c28432eb73b99c" "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/cfe3ed8341a6926caf3ecfa642e275f600799b1c8960a8a3a05f96666cf9281c" "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/bdaf9f6cd83b716006350d9fcf604ade1db193e95a5a56ab00a9dd1a9cf617e8" "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/6a729562400d4c17f030b339f10ed86ef2be3488a1aa91de7ba35bbae014fbde"] :answer-field/correct "q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2/56f884d814aab61b37ad1c11934f96d8d2f7a8aa3ed42a73c9c28432eb73b99c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-93827" :question/id #uuid "4f5f62bb-4839-53ca-a27c-2aa63221765a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93827" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93827/ma-source-field/30cf32b29e154785303a1741714094abaee35186a199382190419454b2d3dba2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
