 {:db/id "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "a620906d-6f92-5060-b907-57c31554da32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :answer-field/correct "q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "b755a996-db20-51e4-9f08-2f14dd2e4212"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$\nII. $8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=10$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-93952/ma-source-field/3e2edded1caae9181035c250aa13120e4b5115f40daa6c94d367094a627962b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
