 {:db/id "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b983f-90c9-5dfc-9769-8b7c27a8d13e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/50b354aeb3d277f3a66bba8e14a84e389adbec1e0db08577d1abba20ce516206" "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/b288cfe6abe26d8eb974b4c95767d18a0f0c14c305ffd885c42b840fe0b5b2ed" "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/b53a060242aa8ab06d0e85af5bfb9d16136158ba7ce0082421b07a9d61864a51" "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/db88281347c452e17bff59fdb81cd0bc4a8657f0566d45591e885df2e9672347" "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/8a1eedf716a96b25c5c153db3528440754c97e33b4b817bfa68d78496968f9ea"] :answer-field/correct "q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5/db88281347c452e17bff59fdb81cd0bc4a8657f0566d45591e885df2e9672347" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-94041" :question/id #uuid "976b5be4-8521-548d-a6b3-f77ad28ed1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94041" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94041/ma-source-field/7cd61fd81cf82a78a02374e96690b0bdf554054c93920e828193f100341e42e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
