 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "f0cabaaa-77b2-5df5-b276-82aad1e2675a"] :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4} & =(0+3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4}{4} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94081/ma-source-field/9822a13a263e5bf9109b314d95d5a0d6f9769a16ae89ae421fd908f0afb63a76"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
