 {:db/id "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "7feb31a0-5779-59d8-9a9b-c01511679c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/24dfb896f7434650058c4068bf7ff779b82571f5fcaa3b71539afeb7e4d590ee" "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/4c21a51594c473dc51f7db2ee81b87c5ec91c43eaf47bd1f79f6d39d0faf4e98" "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/0198a1b6d2d5a44d97d7b3f857e0c5b909a7b8a7f154128ca36221eb1d881850" "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/21614008bfcf882dde490722f7e0902fb7067829d9d9b9d3330e44a2a4fc8252" "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/398913a653bcf169574cc3231871c8974ed229d489ea17ffbefdf7b2ebfa9162"] :answer-field/correct "q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae/21614008bfcf882dde490722f7e0902fb7067829d9d9b9d3330e44a2a4fc8252" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "ca81ca2d-2a11-5281-bdfb-66b5a7ca3f2d"] :question/problem "Lagrange's method can be used to reduce the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ to the canonical form given below. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=\\boxed{} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what is missing from the blank spaces?" :question/worked-solution "We apply Lagrange's method as follows:\n\nFirst, we group ${x}_{1}^{2}$-term with all cross-product terms that contain ${x}_{1}$ and factor out the coefficient of ${x}_{1}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(3{x}_{1}^{2}+18{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3([{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}]-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2})+29{x}_{2}^{2} & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}-27{x}_{2}^{2}+29{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2},\\,{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing parts are as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $3\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94142/ma-source-field/100210e37cb38bdd631805e285cddc656fa8bfa8bb2e117bbbccea32d24404ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
